,"distance":0.4726,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Zac Taylor"},{"season":2023,"team":"WAS","distance":0.7513,"shared":15,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"CIN","distance":0.772,"shared":15,"play_caller":"Zac Taylor"},{"season":2019,"team":"MIA","distance":0.7745,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"SEA","distance":0.784,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Brian Schottenheimer"}],"defense":[{"season":2025,"team":"BUF","distance":0.6866,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2020,"team":"NO","distance":0.8426,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2023,"team":"WAS","distance":0.932,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"NO","distance":0.9755,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2021,"team":"NO","distance":0.9769,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Chiefs went 12-4 in 2019 and outscored opponents 451 to 308. The offense carried the team, ranking 2nd in EPA per play while the defense finished 17th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the highest early-down pass rate and the highest 2-Man rate in the league.","offense":"The Chiefs offense was one of the best in the league by EPA per play, and it was much better passing the ball than running it. They threw more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score did not call for it. Schematically, they stood out for the lowest no-huddle rate and the 3rd lowest 13 personnel usage in the league. Patrick Mahomes ran the passing game, with Travis Kelce leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Chiefs defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the pass than the run, ranking 11th and 28th. Chris Jones was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Frank Clark, who had 8 sacks, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the highest 2-Man rate in the league.","special":"The Chiefs were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 89% of field goals, which was well above average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into receivers, about 17% of the money on the books.","players":"Patrick Mahomes played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,031 yards. Travis Kelce was the clear top target with 24% of the looks and 1,229 receiving yards. The backfield was a committee led by Damien Williams and LeSean McCoy. On defense, Chris Jones led the team with 9 sacks and Tyrann Mathieu picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Andy Reid called the offense as head coach and Steve Spagnuolo called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Bengals under Zac Taylor and the 2023 Commanders. Their defense looked most like the 2025 Bills and the 2020 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 1st in EPA per play in 2018 to 2nd in 2019, about where it was. The defense moved from 23rd in EPA allowed per play in 2018 to 17th in 2019, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was Cover 1 rate, which went from 40% to 23%.","notes":"FTN did not start charting plays until 2022, so play action, motion, RPO and screen rates are not available for 2019. Everything else, including personnel, formations and coverage, is complete for this season."}}